While doing #55499 I noticed that structured kernels didn't really help all that much in reducing duplication, because I still had to do a large amount of throat clearing to assign local variables:
  int64_t pleft = paddingSize[0];
  int64_t pright = paddingSize[1];
  int64_t ptop = paddingSize[2];
  int64_t pbottom = paddingSize[3];
  int64_t pfront = paddingSize[4];
  int64_t pback = paddingSize[5];
  int64_t dimw = 3;
  int64_t dimh = 2;
  int64_t dimd = 1;
  int64_t dimslices = 0;
  int64_t nbatch = 1;

This means we don't really get all that much reduction in code because we have to do this in both cases.  It would be nice if we could preserve these computed values between the two kernels.
One way to permit this preservation would be to make it easier for people to define extra fields in the meta class. For example, all of the variables here could be fields in the meta structured class, and then you would simply set this fields in the meta functions and reference them directly in the impl. We'd need some syntax to specify this in YAML as currently the class entirely codegenerated. One downside is that all of these temporaries will have to spill to memory, even if the optimizer was able to clean things up. Some of the temporaries might just be constexpr things.

In the __torch_function__ documentation an example class is provided:
class LoggingTensor(torch.Tensor):
