@rgommers @hameerabbasi and I have argued about whether or not DisableTorchFunction should be public API or not. Despite being undocumented and unsupported, there have been a few uses of it in the wild https://github.com/search?l=Python&q=disabletorchfunction&type=Code mostly for when people want to delegate to an underlying implementation.
I want to float an alternative idea for how to expose DisableTorchFunction in a user friendly way, and it stems from thinking about the following situation. Suppose you have a Python program that looks like this:
def foo(self):
    if has_torch_function_unary(self):
        return handle_torch_function(foo, (self,), self)
    return bar(self) + bar(self)

def bar(self):
    if has_torch_function_unary(self):
        return handle_torch_function(bar, (self,), self)
    return torch.built_in(self) * 2

Today, if I define a torch function, calling foo always shunts me off to the torch function. I cannot choose to partially delegate the implementation of foo in terms of my implementation of bar; if I re-call the passed in foo on torch function, I'll infinite loop; if I use DisableTorchFunction, I completely disable torch function handling (and just rely on the intrinsic meaning of tensor computation).  What I'd like to do is disable torch function handling only for foo.
This isn't really possible to do in the way we write has torch function today. But a small modification would work:
    if has_torch_function_unary(foo, self):
...
    if has_torch_function_unary(bar, self):

This would allow the has_torch_function to then selectively return false if a torch function advertised that it didn't have support for foo. Although we could do this by adding __has_torch_function__ magic method, it might just be simpler and more obvious if we let DisableTorchFunction(op) take an operator (and then have has_torch_function query it).  The set shouldn't be too large so it should be a few pointer comparisons in the common case.
The main deal is if we do this, we'll have to update all the has_torch_function call sites again. We've done it before, we can probably do it again.
